Recorded real output from an Active Directory domain controller, and the
row-shaped transcription of it that the Directory module's tests replay.

WHERE THESE CAME FROM, exactly, because a fixture whose provenance is unknown is
a fixture that tests whoever wrote it:

    2026-08-27, from the container `os7-ad-dc` — realm OS7.TEST, NetBIOS
    domain OS7, provisioned by installer/testing/ad-dc-entrypoint.sh — running
    samba 4.23.6 (2:4.23.6+dfsg-1ubuntu2.2) on Ubuntu 26.04, on an x64 Windows
    host through Docker Desktop. The client was that same container's
    ldap-utils 2.6.10+dfsg-1ubuntu5.

    Each .ldif is the verbatim stdout of the command named below. Each .json
    beside it is that .ldif transcribed into the row shape
    Invoke-DirectoryRequest returns — @{ Dn; Attributes }, where Attributes
    maps a name to an ARRAY, in the order the server sent it, and objectGUID
    and objectSid stay the base64 the LDIF carries because those are the two
    attributes on these objects that $script:DirectoryBinaryAttributes calls
    binary. Nothing in a .json is interpreted: a userAccountControl is the
    string "514" here, never a decoded flag set.

    Six of the seven were taken over LDAPS, and that is not a preference. A
    simple bind on port 389 is refused by this DC with "Strong authentication
    is required" (measured) — the same finding that makes 636 the module's
    default and 389 opt-in. The rootDSE is readable anonymously, so that one
    was taken on 389.

        export LDAPTLS_CACERT=/var/lib/samba/private/tls/ca.pem
        AD='-x -H ldaps://dc01.os7.test:636
            -D CN=Administrator,CN=Users,DC=os7,DC=test -w Passw0rd-OS7-test
            -b DC=os7,DC=test -LLL -o ldif-wrap=no'

    rootdse.ldif             ldapsearch -x -H ldap://localhost -s base -b "" \
                                        -LLL -o ldif-wrap=no
    user-enabled.ldif        ldapsearch $AD "(sAMAccountName=os7fixture1)"
    user-disabled.ldif       ldapsearch $AD "(sAMAccountName=os7fixture2)"
    group-one-member.ldif    ldapsearch $AD "(sAMAccountName=OS7FixtureGroup)"
    group-empty.ldif         ldapsearch $AD "(sAMAccountName=OS7FixtureEmpty)"
    computer.ldif            ldapsearch $AD "(sAMAccountName=OS7FIXTUREPC\$)"
    ou.ldif                  ldapsearch $AD "(ou=OS7Fixtures)"

    That password is the lab default out of ad-dc-entrypoint.sh and is already
    in the tree; it unlocks a container and nothing else. `-LLL` is why there
    is no `# extended LDIF` banner and no `# search result` footer, and
    `-o ldif-wrap=no` is why no line is continued — which is the only reason
    the .json files could be produced mechanically rather than typed.

    THOSE COMMAND LINES WERE RECONSTRUCTED, NOT RECORDED. Whoever captured the
    .ldif files left no capture script behind, so every line above was re-run
    against the same DC on 2026-08-27 and its output compared with the file it
    is claimed to have produced. Six are byte-for-byte identical. rootdse.ldif
    differs in exactly two lines, currentTime and highestCommittedUSN, which
    cannot be equal across two runs of anything. A command nobody has re-run
    is a command somebody remembers typing.

    The transcription itself was checked against a witness that shares no code
    with the LDAP client: `ldbsearch --show-binary` reads sam.ldb directly and
    renders user-enabled's objectSid as
    S-1-5-21-2765228506-4257399916-1448377294-1105 and its objectGUID as
    d42494ae-7f6f-490a-9b59-177c0844a0b8. The base64 in user-enabled.json
    decodes to both. `net getdomainsid` agrees on the domain half.

WHAT THEY COVER, AND WHAT THEY DO NOT.

Seven objects, and each one is a state some piece of the module branches on:

  * A one-member group and an empty one — the #92 pair. group-one-member has a
    single `member` value, the case where a bare `return @($values)` unwraps to
    a [string] and the caller's `[0]` indexes a character. group-empty has no
    `member` KEY AT ALL rather than an empty one: a missing attribute is an
    absent key, not a present key with nothing in it, and under Set-StrictMode
    reaching for it is a terminating error rather than $null.
  * userAccountControl in three states: 512 (enabled), 514 (512 | 0x2
    ACCOUNTDISABLE) and 4098 (0x1000 WORKSTATION_TRUST | 0x2) on the computer.
    Enough to prove ACCOUNTDISABLE decodes, and nothing beyond it.
  * accountExpires 9223372036854775807 on all three accounts —
    0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, which means "never" and NOT a date, and which a naive
    FILETIME conversion renders as the year 30828 or overflows outright.
  * pwdLastSet in both of its forms: a real FILETIME on each user — two
    different ones, so a converter cannot pass by matching a constant — and 0
    on the computer, which also means "never" and also is not 1601.
  * A computer account whose sAMAccountName ends in `$`, the one character
    here that has to survive an RFC 4515 filter and a shell on the way to it.
  * An OU, which has no objectSid at all: the binary-attribute path has to
    cope with the attribute simply not being present.
  * A rootDSE advertising supportedLDAPVersion 2 AND 3. The server offers
    both, and .NET's LdapConnection defaults to 2 (BUILD-NOTES #94) — so the
    fixture that would let a connection talk LDAPv2 to a server happy to
    answer it is sitting right here.
  * 23 supportedControl values of which only 15 are DISTINCT:
    1.2.840.113556.1.4.1413 appears six times and 1.2.840.113556.1.4.801
    three. A directory attribute is a multi-valued list and not a set, and a
    check that counts controls gets a different answer from one that dedupes
    them.
  * The `# ref` lines at the foot of six files are real continuation
    references — three on every search against the domain root, which is
    measurement 6 in Directory.psm1's header arriving as evidence rather than
    as a claim. They are NOT part of a row: Invoke-DirectoryRequest puts them
    on the response's Referrals, and the .json files, being rows, do not carry
    them. A test of referral handling has to read them out of the .ldif.

What they do not cover, in this directory, today:

  * PAGING. The paged-results control 1.2.840.113556.1.4.319 is advertised in
    rootdse.ldif and not one fixture exercises it — every command above
    matched exactly one entry. There is no cookie here, no second page and no
    union of two pages; AD truncates at MaxPageSize (1000) with no error and
    no exception, just a short list.
  * A CN containing a comma. Every DN here splits on commas cleanly, so
    ConvertTo-DirectoryDnValue's "Lovelace, Ada" case is constructed in the
    self-test and has to be labelled as constructed, not measured.
  * Any failure at all. A bad bind is an LdapException, and an exception
    cannot be written down in LDIF, so nothing here carries one.

A .json is ONE row, not a list. A replay must build the array at the call site
— `@(fixture)` — because handing the single [pscustomobject] back bare is
exactly the shape #92 describes.

AND A REPLAY MUST DECODE THE BASE64 BEFORE IT HANDS A ROW TO THE MODULE.
Invoke-DirectoryRequest returns byte[] for the attributes in
$script:DirectoryBinaryAttributes; these files hold base64, because base64 is
what the LDIF holds and the evidence is not worth re-encoding. So a fixture
loaded verbatim gives objectSid as a [string], the module's converters cast it
with [byte[]], and the result is not a SID — it is a different failure from the
one being tested. Measured 2026-08-27: after
[Convert]::FromBase64String, user-enabled.json driven through the real seam
comes back out of ConvertFrom-DirectorySid and ConvertFrom-DirectoryGuid as
S-1-5-21-2765228506-4257399916-1448377294-1105 and
d42494ae-7f6f-490a-9b59-177c0844a0b8 — the two values ldbsearch prints. That
round trip is the reason to trust these transcriptions at all.

WHAT IS STILL OWED, and must come from real hardware rather than being written
by hand here:

THESE CAME FROM SAMBA, AND SAMBA IS NOT WINDOWS SERVER. A green run against
this DC says less than it looks like it says, and the gap does not close by
adding fixtures — it is a different implementation of the same protocol. What
Samba will not reproduce:

  * Channel binding and LDAP signing enforcement. A Windows Server DC with
    LdapEnforceChannelBinding=2 / LDAPServerIntegrity=2 refuses binds this DC
    accepts, and SessionOptions.Sealing and .Signing THROW on Linux
    (measured), so there is no way to satisfy such a DC from here at all. The
    single Microsoft constraint most likely to decide whether this feature
    works at a customer is the one nothing in this directory can exercise.
  * Windows password-policy sub-codes. AD answers rc 49 for every credential
    failure, and only the `data 52e` / `525` / `530` / `532` / `533` / `701` /
    `775` sub-code separates a wrong password from an account that is locked
    out, expired, or outside its logon hours. Samba's coverage and wording of
    those are Samba's.
  * The msDS-* constructed attributes — msDS-UserPasswordExpiryTimeComputed,
    msDS-User-Account-Control-Computed and the rest. Not one appears in any
    file here: they are computed by the DC on request, and Samba computes a
    different set from a different schema.
  * LAPS. No msLAPS-* attribute exists on this DC to be read.
  * Cross-forest referrals. The three references recorded here all point back
    into os7.test. A referral that crosses a trust is the thing
    ReferralChasing=None exists to make visible, and a single-domain lab
    cannot produce one.

A REAL WINDOWS SERVER DOMAIN CONTROLLER IS OWED. Until one has answered these
same queries, the files in this directory describe how Samba renders Active
Directory, not how Active Directory behaves.

Cheaper than that, and also missing:

  * A LOCKED-OUT ACCOUNT. No fixture carries lockoutTime — user-disabled is
    disabled and has never been locked — so the 49/775 path has no recorded
    object behind it.
  * A search returning MORE THAN ONE entry, and one returning more than 1000.
    Both are the paging gap above, and both are #89: a sort that has only ever
    seen one element has not been tested.
  * A SECOND domain controller, for exactly the same reason.
  * A DC whose certificate is signed by something other than itself, so the
    LDAPS trust path is exercised against a CA an administrator would really
    install rather than the self-signed one this container generates.
